139	{'parameter_id': 3, 'term_id': 'TERM-001', 'status': 'active', 'merged_into': None, 'notes': 'Re-run of the cleared circulation corpus. TERM-001 is base vocabulary minted 2026-05-09, so this is a PROMOTION, not a coining: add-term/observe-term is deliberately not used, which would have coined a near-duplicate beside the existing term.', 'created_at': '2026-09-16 04:55', 'created_by_session': 'session_2026-09-16-research-batch-08-ramp-gradient', 'updated_at': '2026-09-17 22:13', 'updated_by_session': 'session_2026-09-17-engine-proxy-inference', 'direction_rationale': 'A gentler ramp is more accessible, and the admitted evidence states it rather than the engine assuming it. REF-00996 (Handboek Toegankelijkheid) gives both the criterion and its mechanism: hoe groter het hoogteverschil, hoe zachter de helling moet zijn, omdat men gedurende een langere tijd een inspanning moet leveren — the greater the rise, the gentler the ramp must be, because the effort must be sustained longer — with the physical feasibility of the independently self-propelling wheelchair user as the starting point. REF-00987 (ADA) says it in its own advisory to 405.2: provide ramps with the least possible running slope. REF-00994 (IPC) frames 1:20 as best practice and 1:14 as a concession for ancillary facilities only, which is the same direction stated as a preference ordering. The T1 dose-response rows measure it: articular discomfort and pushrim force both rise with gradient across the tested range. Four sources, three strata, one direction, none contradicted — derived, not adjudicated. NOT contested: no admitted source identifies a population for whom a steeper ramp is better. Recorded because compose_value cannot select most-accommodatingly without it and was returning a NULL interval with that exact complaint.', 'accessibility_direction': 'lower_is_better'}
